hi my name is galiona I'll be presenting  our paper decision making under  miscalibration this is Joint work with  guy Rathbone and unfortunately I  couldn't be here in person so apologies  in advance  okay so the setting we study in this  work we call it from predictions to  Decisions by predictions I mean that we  have an individuals a universe of  individuals X and some risk predictor  which is a mapping from X to values in  the interval zero one for example to  estimate the risk of a cardiovascular  event in some follow-up period by  decisions I mean that the way we intend  to use B is to inform multiple  Downstream binary decision problems for  example whether an individual should be  prescribed a medication or be given a  certain medical treatment  so we treat this in a utility-based  framework basically we assume we have  some distribution over individual and  outcome pairs a risk predictor as I  mentioned a treatment threshold is just  some number J in the interval 0 1 where  we understand that someone receives a  treatment if their prediction is higher  than this value and a downstream  decision problem is basically  parameterized by its cost structure so  think of it as the relative costs of  making correct versus incorrect  predictions so overall all of these  components Define a utility from  threshold in P at a threshold J  and we will say that the treatment is  harmful if this utility is actually  lower than what we could have obtained  without having used the predictor at all  right we can always just decide to treat  everyone or treat no one and that gives  us some sense of utility as well  okay so for us the decision making  problem is basically given the cost the  distribution and the predictor how  should we choose the treatment threshold  J  and an existing but partial answer to  this question is that whenever the  predictor is perfectly calibrated then  there is a threshold but I'll notice J  star and in the literature is known as  the therapeutic threshold which  guarantees no harm in this case  and the interesting property about J  star is that it does not depend on  either the distribution or the predictor  at all only on the cost structure of the  decision problem  and here is the perfect calibration  requirement is basically saying that for  every possible risk value V if we look  at all the individuals that receive the  prediction of Z exactly the fraction of  these actually have positive outcomes in  the true distribution  um unfortunately this result breaks down  under miscalibration so for example in  this previous work the authors used  simulation studies to show that whenever  they induce miscalibration to a  calibrated predictor P eventually it  makes treating at this therapeutic  threshold are actually harmful in the  sense that I've defined before  so let's start gaining some intuition  for how decision making might look like  under miscalibration so supposedly the  therapeutic threshold here is 0.05  intuitively this means that I anticipate  a much greater benefit from identifying  true positives than having false  positive predictions  and consider the say the subset of women  who receive a prediction of 0.04  should I treat these or should I not  treat these so if I knew that my  predictor was perfectly calibrated on  this subgroup then I know that I should  not treat them but what happens under  miscalibration so I could still use J  star and not read them but then if my  predictor was underestimating the risk  in this population I might regret not  treating them but on the other hand if I  use a lower threshold and then find out  that my predictor was overestimating the  risk in this group then I might regret  treating them so overall we have these  competing concerns and it's not a priori  clear how to balance them because the  impact of the errors that I can have  within a certain bound of a  miscalibration errors don't have  symmetric effects because of the kind of  asymmetric nature 